[p149]        6.1.     PROP-001 Pricing Proposal Definition
[p149] 
[p149] 
[p149] A Pricing Proposal must represent a specific proposed pricing action associated with one
[p149] Pricing Initiative.
[p149] 
[p149] The Proposal must answer, at minimum:
[p149] 
[p149]     ● what pricing action is being proposed;
[p149]     ● which priceable entities and pricing dimensions are affected;
[p149]     ● what the relevant current or baseline pricing position is;
[p149]     ● what the proposed pricing position or rule is;
[p149]     ● why the action is being proposed;
[p149]     ● what material assumptions support it;
[p149]     ● what analysis supports the action;
[p149]     ● which applicable guardrails were evaluated;
[p149]     ● when the proposed action should become effective.
[p149] 
[p149] A Pricing Proposal must not represent the entire Pricing Initiative.
[p149] 
[p149]

[p149]        6.2.     PROP-002 Initiative Relationship
[p149] 
[p150] Every Pricing Proposal must belong to exactly one Pricing Initiative.
[p150] 
[p150] The Pricing Initiative must preserve the relationship between the Proposal and the
[p150] underlying:
[p150] 
[p150]     ● pricing objective;
[p150]     ● pricing scope;
[p150]     ● pricing context;
[p150]     ● Pricing Builder work;
[p150]     ● pricing logic;
[p150]     ● analysis;
[p150]     ● Scenarios where applicable;
[p150]     ● assumptions;
[p150]     ● evidence;
[p150]     ● collaboration;
[p150]     ● workflow;
[p150]     ● organisational decision records, including approvals and policy authorisations;
[p150]     ● execution records.
[p150] 
[p150] A Pricing Proposal must not exist as disconnected pricing work outside its originating Pricing
[p150] Initiative.
[p150] 
[p150]

[p151]        6.5.     PROP-005 Proposal Identification
[p151] 
[p151] 
[p151] Every Pricing Proposal must have a persistent identifier within its Pricing Initiative.
[p151] 
[p151] Every controlled Proposal version must also have a unique version identifier.
[p151] 
[p151] The system must preserve, at minimum:
[p151] 
[p151]     ● Proposal identifier;
[p151]     ● Proposal version;
[p151]     ● originating Pricing Initiative;
[p151] 
[p152]    ● current or historical status;
[p152]    ● creator;
[p152]    ● creation timestamp;
[p152]    ● submitting user where applicable;
[p152]    ● submission timestamp where applicable.
[p152] 
[p152] Users must be able to distinguish the current Proposal version from previous versions.
[p152] 
[p152]

[p152]       6.6.    PROP-006 Proposal Content
[p152] 
[p152] 
[p152] Before formal submission, the Pricing Proposal must contain sufficient information for an
[p152] authorised reviewer or approver to understand the proposed pricing decision without
[p152] reconstructing it from disconnected parts of the Initiative.
[p152] 
[p152] Where applicable, the Proposal must contain or reference:
[p152] 
[p152]    ● affected pricing scope;
[p152]    ● current or baseline pricing state;
[p152]    ● proposed pricing action;
[p152]    ● proposed prices or pricing logic;
[p152]    ● relevant currency;
[p152]    ● pricing objective;
[p152]    ● supporting rationale;
[p152]    ● material assumptions;
[p152]    ● relevant analysis and calculated outputs;
[p152]    ● applicable guardrail evaluation;
[p152]    ● material exceptions;
[p152]    ● relevant supporting evidence;
[p152]    ● intended effective date or effective period;
[p152]    ● relevant implementation information required for operational handoff.
[p152] 
[p152] Information referenced from another Initiative object must remain bound to the exact version
[p152] used by the Proposal.
[p152] 
[p152]

[p152]       6.7.    PROP-007 Proposal Source Traceability
[p152] 
[p152] 
[p152] The Proposal must preserve traceability to the pricing work from which it was created.
[p152] 
[p152] Where applicable, users must be able to determine:
[p152] 
[p153]    ● which Pricing Builder logic produced the proposed action;
[p153]    ● which Scenario was selected;
[p153]    ● which calculations informed the Proposal;
[p153]    ● which assumptions were used;
[p153]    ● which pricing-context values were relied upon;
[p153]    ● which evidence supported the rationale;
[p153]    ● which guardrails were evaluated.
[p153] 
[p153] The system must not silently replace these sources after the Proposal has been submitted.
[p153] 
[p153]

[p162]     6.22.     PROP-022 Proposal Historical Integrity
[p162] 
[p162] 
[p162] Every consequential Pricing Proposal must remain historically understandable after the
[p162] Pricing Initiative progresses or is completed.
[p162] 
[p162] For each Proposal version, an authorised user should be able to determine:
[p162] 
[p162]    ● what was proposed;
[p162]    ● which Pricing Initiative it belonged to;
[p162]    ● which pricing scope it affected;
[p162]    ● what baseline or current pricing state was used;
[p162]    ● what pricing action was proposed;
[p162]    ● which assumptions and analysis supported it;
[p162]    ● which guardrails applied;
[p162]    ● which evidence supported the decision;
[p162]    ● who submitted it;
[p162]    ● which version was submitted;
[p162] 
[p163]    ● what decision was made;
[p163]    ● who made the decision;
[p163]    ● whether changes were requested or the Proposal was rejected;
[p163]    ● whether the Proposal was Approved or Authorised Within Policy;
[p163]    ● whether the version later became Superseded or Expired;
[p163]    ● whether it became the basis for execution.
[p163] 
[p163] If the exact decision represented by a historical Proposal version cannot be reconstructed
[p163] from Owómidé, the Proposal record is incomplete.
[p163] 
[p164] 3.7 Collaboration and Evidence
[p164] Management
[p164] 
[p165]                 7. COLLABORATION AND EVIDENCE MANAGEMENT
[p165] The MVP must allow relevant stakeholders to collaborate directly around pricing work while
[p165] preserving the relationship between each contribution and the Pricing Initiative, analysis,
[p165] scenario, proposal or decision it concerns.
[p165] 
[p165] Collaboration should reduce off-platform coordination without attempting to replace general
[p165] workplace communication tools.
[p165] 
